3104] releases also carry an [l26126] logo. In these cases [l26126] should not be added as a label, since the boxed [l26126]-logo that appears with the [l13104] label merely implies that [l13104] formed part of the [l70023], not that [l26126] was a label itself\".[/b]\r\n","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:13104","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":111596,"name":"Hispavox, S.A."},"sublabels":[{"id":1423197,"name":"Ases Del Fandango"},{"id":2042233,"name":"Con El Paso Del Tiempo"},{"id":3600601,"name":"Estilos Flamencos"},{"id":2039178,"name":"Grabaciones Historicas"},{"id":742138,"name":"Grandes Canciones De"},{"id":767393,"name":"La Revista"},{"id":781859,"name":"Los Grandes Exitos De La Zarzuela"},{"id":913239,"name":"Maestros De La Guitarra"},{"id":628369,"name":"Mira Las Laminas Escucha El Disco Lee El Libro"},{"id":1136433,"name":"Obras Maestras De La Música Española"},{"id":976910,"name":"Premio Nacional De Música 1995"},{"id":1129721,"name":"Trésors Du Flamenco"},{"id":404714,"name":"Zzelesta"}]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/13104-Hispavox","type":"label","urls":["http://es.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hispavox","http://www.facebook.com/Hispavox/timeline?filter=1"]}},"/api/discogs/label/1398/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1398","image":"https://i.discogs.com/aW7soD2h7a3UwfLySdkKxNSW7awQAsCNlVqmIFnC_lQ/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:206/w:300/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTEzOTgt/MTM1MzA4MTExOC04/NDkxLmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/zvytO2xlCaGdg1uaLyLkkLCAz0Fa8CmAehvnmlcP7Zw/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTEzOTgt/MTM1MzA4MTExOC04/NDkxLmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Electronic music label based in Italy, run by Enrico Crivellaro alias [a=Volcov].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1398","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":807,"name":"Archive (2)"},"sublabels":[{"id":2323195,"name":"Neroli From The Archive"}]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/1398-Neroli","type":"label","urls":["https://soundcloud.com/neroli","https://www.residentadvisor.net/record-label.aspx?id=10302","https://nerolirecords.bandcamp.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/label/1605991/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"1605991","image":"https://i.discogs.com/XoHZ2QiWLaTnkPPA_xg1xIJC-JxKJU346eqbDjVuNNw/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:669/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE2MDU5/OTEtMTU3MTIxMDg3/MC00MzQwLnBuZw.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/nYdOwi_9epUQI2AMGUWkqX38IjlrjH-jAcsGi73Xxiw/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTE2MDU5/OTEtMTU3MTIxMDg3/MC00MzQwLnBuZw.jpeg","profile":"Danish label operated by [a=DJ Popup]","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:1605991","relations":{"parentLabel":null,"sublabels":null},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/1605991-NES-3","type":"label","urls":["https://nespace.dk/","https://safedist.ro/","https://nespace.bandcamp.com/"]}},"/api/discogs/label/26011/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"26011","image":"https://i.discogs.com/tF70WtpYNAjxBywWbB0nb4EWKDg5bq4rlESFA29P7oM/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:600/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTI2MDEx/LTE0ODQ1NjE1OTUt/Mzg0MC5qcGVn.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/K-fKCcP8Sk3EwVMOURotjG4uUwHZTIZUHLZdG3qkx-s/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTI2MDEx/LTE0ODQ1NjE1OTUt/Mzg0MC5qcGVn.jpeg","profile":"US label.\n[b]Label Code: LC 9173 / LC 09173[/b]\nThe label was established in 1981 by [a=Tom Silverman] when the entrepreneurial trade mag publisher began releasing the best of the demos crossing his path. He struck gold with the revolutionary debut by Afrika Bambaataa & The Soul Sonic Force - Planet Rock. One of the only 12\" singles ever to sell 600,000+ copies, it laid the foundation for the genre-defining house that Tommy built. The original TB logo featured 3 break-dancers that were photographed and then edited into a cameo style. They were Michael “Mr. Frost” Diaz of the Hot Feet Rockers, Dicko “Solo” Souleymane of the Paris City Rockers and Tommy Braddock of the Pure Energy Crew.  Tommy Boy and its stable of stars led the way as the first wave of hip-hop's artistic translation of urban street culture became a mass-appeal phenomenon. With repeated high-charting crossover success, Tommy Boy earned almost 80 gold and platinum citations, and the label's groundbreaking grooves are essential pillars of hip-hop, as evidenced in this new collection of many of their greatest early tracks.\n\nIn 1985, Warner Bros. Records acquired 50% of the label and made Tom Silverman and Monica Lynch vice presidents of its new partner. However, Tommy Boy preferred to use independent distribution instead of the distribution channels owned by Warner Bros. Records' parent, Warner Music (then WEA Corp.). Five years later, Warner Bros. acquired the remaining 50% of the label. In 1995, following the controversy following the backlash against Warner Music’s gangsta artists, Silverman bought back 50% of his label. In February 2002, because Warner Music wanted larger unit sales, Tom Silverman bought back the remaining half of Tommy Boy and made it independent again. As part of the break-up, Warner Music was given first dibs to acquire the contracts of the artists signed to the label and became owner of the master recordings that Tommy Boy released until that date.\n\nIn 2017, Tommy Boy reacquired its catalogue, plus the catalogues of Brand Nubian, Grand Puba, and Club Nouveau, as a result of WEA divestments because of the latter’s purchase of Parlophone.\n\nNote:\nHistorically, this label was not to be used on Discogs; releases bearing the Tommy Boy logo were only to be filed under either [l=Tommy Boy Music] or [l=Tommy Boy Entertainment]. Now, however, it is appropriate to use it as the label for releases bearing the Tommy Boy logo. Additional companies may still be entered.","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:26011","relations":{"parentLabel":{"id":613350,"name":"Reservoir Media Management"},"sublabels":[{"id":4736,"name":"75 Ark"},{"id":23321,"name":"Body Rock"},{"id":1031896,"name":"Freestyle Greatest Beats"},{"id":75595,"name":"Silver Label"},{"id":10575,"name":"StepSun Music Entertainment"},{"id":552816,"name":"Timber!"},{"id":287621,"name":"Tommy Boy 20th Anniversary Series"},{"id":28650,"name":"Tommy Boy Black Label"},{"id":111924,"name":"Tommy Boy Gospel"},{"id":237210,"name":"Tommy Boy Greatest Beats"},{"id":4060,"name":"Tommy Boy Silver Label"}]},"schemaVersion":2,"sourceUrl":"https://www.discogs.com/label/26011-Tommy-Boy","type":"label","urls":["https://www.tommyboy.com/","https://tommyboyrecords.bandcamp.com/","https://www.instagram.com/tommyboyrecords/","https://twitter.com/tommyboyrecords","https://www.facebook.com/TommyBoyRecords","https://soundcloud.com/tommyboy","https://www.youtube.com/user/TommyBoyEnt","https://www.youtube.com/TommyBoy","https://www.youtube.com/@TommyBoyRecords","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tommy_Boy_Records"]}},"/api/discogs/label/4898/profile":{"cached":true,"profile":{"id":"4898","image":"https://i.discogs.com/dHyVAaNn4b_Gbv05fR1JFDZYAmyzXHpIy7G-AQmCdto/rs:fit/g:sm/q:90/h:600/w:561/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTQ4OTgt/MTQxNDA0NDQ3MC0x/NjY3LmpwZWc.jpeg","imageThumb":"https://i.discogs.com/AZHItifKQeJrvABcEge0VyvXOVllFgJ0s1KE1wtixig/rs:fit/g:sm/q:40/h:150/w:150/czM6Ly9kaXNjb2dz/LWRhdGFiYXNlLWlt/YWdlcy9MLTQ4OTgt/MTQxNDA0NDQ3MC0x/NjY3LmpwZWc.jpeg","profile":"Multinational label of US origin; also appears as \"[i]United Artists[/i]\" or \"[i]UA[/i]\" only. Label code: LC 0379 / LC 00379.\r\nFor all unofficial / bootleg release of this label please see [l161866].\r\nFor the record company see [l=United Artists Records Inc.], for the publishing company see [l=United Artists Music Co., Inc.]\r\n\r\n[b]History[/b]\r\n1958: United Artists Records, a subsidiary of the United Artists film studio, was founded by movie producer [a=Max E. Youngstein] initially to distribute records of its own soundtracks. It soon branched out into recording music of a number of different genres.\r\n1960: [l=United Artists Ultra Audio], a label for special wide ranged stereo recording, was introduced. US mono releases commonly mention the [l=Wall To Wall Sound] series & the cat# prefix WW. US stereo releases [l=Wall To Wall Stereo] series and the  WWS cat# prefix.\r\n1967: In May the [l=Transamerica Corporation] acquired 95,7 % of [l=United Artists Records, Inc.], as mentioned on the front page of Billboard 30. Sept. 1967. The 'Unart' label was revived as a budget label in September.\r\n1968: [l=Liberty] bought by [l=Transamerica Corporation] in June. Both labels got under one umbrella now.\r\n1971: [l=Liberty] was shut down and merged into UA.\r\n1979: After successful years in the 1970s, especially in the jazz and mainstream pop branches, United Artists Records was bought by UA executives [a=Artie Mogull] and Jerry Rubinstein from the holding company [l=Transamerica Corporation]. When United Artists Records lost the distribution deal with [l=Jet Records] and with it the whole sales of the [a=Electric Light Orchestra] in the same year, the company was swallowed by the [l=EMI Group].\r\n1980: [l=EMI] revived the [l=Liberty] label, transferred all UA-Artists and closed down the label in the same year.  \r\n\r\n[b]International[/b]\r\nBelgium: represented by [l=S.A. Fonior N.V.] between ca. 1961-1964, afterwards by [l=S.A. Gramophone N.V.]\r\nNetherlands: manufactured by [l=Philips Phonografische Industrie] between ca. 1960-1963, represented by [l=Artone Gramofoonplaten] between 1964-1969 and by [l=Bovema] afterwards.\r\nUK: distributed by [l=E.M.I. Records Limited] until approximately middle of 1968, when the company changed to [l=Liberty Records Limited].","provider":"discogs","ref":"discogs:label:4898","relations":{"parentLabel":null,"sublabels":[{"id":1637576,"name":"50 Fabulous Favorites"},{"id":437182,"name":"Anthology Of Re